          "heading": "Engineering Design Project & Numerical Investigation: Power Semiconductor Devices, Switching Characteristics, and Conduction Losses",
          "content": "# Unit 1 design study \u2014 Portable solar-supply power budget\n\n**Objective.** Show that the reader can connect switching equations to a bounded engineering requirement, rather than only repeat a nominal calculation.\n\n## Scenario and data\n\nBuild a synthetic operating-point table for 18 V, 24 V and 30 V sources feeding a 12 V output. At each source voltage use load currents 0.5, 2 and 4 A. Compare a fixed 0.6 W controller loss with 0.12 \u03a9 times squared output current; compute efficiency and missing heat. Calculate input current from energy balance rather than assuming it equals load current.\n\nUse the table to identify the operating point with highest total heat and the point with poorest efficiency. Explain why those need not be identical. On the model plot, annotate whether the efficiency calculation includes gate driver, wiring and thermal feedback. Compare the result against a deliberately unrealistic claim of 99.9% efficiency and determine what uncertainty would be necessary to defend such a value.\n\n## Engineering notebook procedure\n\n1. **Requirements table.** Write columns for demanded function, allowed input range, load range, maximum stress, accuracy tolerance, missing information and evidence. Reject adjectives without an associated test. Where values have not been provided, label them 'to be determined' and explain the measurement or datasheet that will resolve them.\n2. **Energy and switching state diagram.** Give the current path in each interval and show inductor voltage or capacitor current with consistent sign conventions. State which approximation lets the calculation use cycle averages. If conduction mode can change, identify the condition at which the derivation stops being applicable.\n3. **Hand calculation.** Carry through at least one numerical case from this unit's lessons, report intermediate values with units, check a limiting case, and recompute it with a 20% parameter variation. Separate assumed input values from computed results and ensure no conclusion depends on a hidden choice of waveform convention.\n4. **Comparison with a different model.** Run the companion offline simulation at three operating points; record how its assumptions differ from the hand calculation. The model must not be described as validated physical hardware. If the simulation refuses an impossible operating point, that refusal is a finding rather than a reason to bypass the guardrail.\n5. **Failure analysis.** Identify at least two independent failures: one electrical stress or thermal failure and one sensing, timing, protection or measurement failure. For each propose an observable symptom and a method that could falsify the simplified design claim.\n6. **Review and decision.** Assemble a one-page design summary with chosen assumptions, calculated quantities, sensitivity, unresolved safety risks, measurement uncertainty and a justified next test. Do not specify live-mains experiments or unsupervised high-energy hardware as part of this project.\n\n## Evaluation rubric (20 points)\n\n- Four points: correct switching-state or control-volume representation with reference directions.\n- Four points: independently checkable arithmetic, correct SI units and a valid domain of application.\n- Four points: design tradeoff and a nonnominal sensitivity study.\n- Four points: an explicit falsification or failure test and appropriate safety bounds.\n- Four points: clear interpretation of simulation limitations and remaining uncertainty.\n\n## Instructor checkpoint\n\nA submission that uses the right numerical formula with the wrong conduction regime cannot receive full credit. Similarly, a convincing waveform plot does not compensate for missing component limits. The strongest submissions explain what would change their conclusion. Ask learners to present a one-minute defense of the least reliable assumption and to identify the next measurement or calculation that is most likely to overturn the result.\n\n## Optional deeper investigation\n\nVary temperature, parasitic resistance or sensor delay separately. Compare the relative importance of each effect at light load versus rated load. Use an uncertainty table rather than drawing more decimal places. Explain if the most important uncertainty is irreducible without physical measurement, or whether a manufacturer specification supplies a conservative bound. Finally, distinguish the *verified* result\u2014perhaps an algebraic equality or a checked simulator calculation\u2014from an *unverified* implication about real hardware.\n\n**Safety boundary:** EDUCATIONAL MODEL ONLY. Do not connect mains voltage or high-energy batteries to a classroom prototype. A schematic, component value, or simulation in this volume is not a certified electrical-safety design. Use isolated, current-limited training hardware only under competent supervision and follow relevant safety rules, ratings, and test procedures.\n"
