          "statement": "For a 48 V bus, 5 A current and 40 ns estimated turn-on plus 60 ns turn-off overlap, E_total\u22480.5\u00d748\u00d75\u00d7100 ns=12 microjoules per cycle. At 100 kHz this is 1.2 W. A doubled switching frequency roughly doubles that term if waveforms remain otherwise unchanged.",
          "solution": "**How to verify the arithmetic independently.** First convert every milliohm, microsecond, microfarad, kilohertz and microhenry into consistent base units. Next substitute the stated values into the governing relation in one line before rounding. Keep a separate column for the measured or assumed quantity and the calculated quantity. After calculation, check the physical limiting case: at zero load there should be no load power, at zero switching interval there should not be energy transfer through an ideal PWM command, and the sign of a voltage cannot be changed by efficiency alone. These simple limiting checks often expose a wrong topology or an invalid formula more reliably than another decimal place.\n\nFor a useful second calculation, change one design variable while holding the others fixed. If a capacitor is changed, do not simultaneously alter switching frequency and load current, because the effect becomes difficult to attribute. Record which quantities are genuinely controlled and which are consequences of the change. Where the model is an approximation, report its domain of validity beside the number; a precise numerical answer derived outside the model's domain can be more misleading than a rough answer with honest uncertainty."
