          "solution": "**Diagnostic challenge.** Why can a diode that is cool at 20 kHz run much hotter at 200 kHz without an increased load current? Write one observable signal whose change would support the diagnosis and another whose change would contradict it. State whether the correct experiment needs a cycle-average, RMS, peak or instantaneous waveform measurement.\n\n**Reference answer and marking guidance.** The original case is as follows: An average 4 A diode current with an assumed constant 0.65 V forward drop dissipates 2.6 W in the simple model. If reverse-recovery energy is 6 microjoules per transition at 80 kHz, its associated average loss is 0.48 W, giving 3.08 W total under these chosen assumptions. A correct solution must restate the equation or conservation argument before evaluating it. The requested 10% extension depends on the chosen parameter and must state what is held constant. A response that ignores the limitation\u2014Average conduction current multiplied by a single forward drop is a simplification; actual loss requires current waveform and V_F(I,T). Reverse-recovery energy is not necessarily independent of current, temperature or dI/dt.\u2014is incomplete. Award credit for identifying the correct domain, not for inventing physical readings that were never measured."
